exec(open('work/scripts/research_notes.py').read().split("if __name__=='__main__':")[0])
s='V. Elementary formal structures, inverse functions and integration'
add('N60',s,39,'003–004; 010–016','The axiom labels change between the two drafts','reading question',r'The first draft begins “$k$ un … corps”, while scan 010 restarts with “$k$ anneau commutatif”; composition, inversion, differentiation and integration also receive different letter labels in the two presentations. In particular, the old f) is inverse-function closure, whereas the later f) is an integral quotient condition, and scan 012 itself has an uncertain d)/e) label. Statements should therefore be compared by content and hypotheses, not by a bare reference to a), b), c), d) or f).',['HENS','S19'],'adds a version distinction needed to interpret 39-inverse-function-axioms-henselian','inverse-function|axiom|henselian')
add('N61',s,39,'003','An uncertain target in the first unit axiom','reading question',r'In c), the first ink-based reading of the conclusion is “$1/f\in S_n$”, with $\mathcal A_n$ retained as an alternative; the following c′) and the examples suggest that closure inside $\mathcal A_n$ was intended. The two possibilities have very different mathematical force, so the implication should not be used as if the stronger target were an unambiguous transcription.',['HENS'],'adds an earlier source ambiguity; the Hua entry concerns the later draft and does not resolve this reading','inverse-function|unit|henselian')
add('N62',s,39,'004','Henselianity versus closure under all formal inverse maps','self-flagged',r'The margin asks “f′) implique-t-il f (moyennant a) à e))? il ne semble pas”, after “et est équivalente à f′” has been deleted. This is a family-level converse question: individual henselianity of the $\mathcal A_n$ may not by itself control the inverse coordinates in the required number of variables. The supplied Hua claim addresses the forward direction and therefore supplies no answer to this explicit converse question.',['HENS'],'differs from the forward implication in 39-inverse-function-axioms-henselian; adds the unresolved converse','inverse-function|henselian|converse')
add('N63',s,39,'004','The minimal henselian family is a test case, not a converse proof','unproved claim',r'The note that the minimal family “$B_n=P\{t_1,\ldots,t_n\}$” satisfies “e) et f)” calls for compatibility of henselizations with substitution and inverse coordinate changes. Even if this example is proved, it does not establish the preceding converse for every admissible family; the deleted attempt and the uncertain margin “vicié? / inutile” should not be promoted to that missing general proof.',['HENS'],'adds a precise test family without settling the converse absent from the context','inverse-function|henselian|minimal')
add('N64',s,39,'005','Time-dependent flow closure is reduced to an autonomous system','unproved claim',r'The equivalence of g) and g′) is attributed to “la variable temps devenant variable spatiale.” The proposed construction is the autonomous field $\partial_t+\xi(t,x)$ on the product with the time axis, followed by restriction to initial time zero; a proof must check that its coefficients, its flow, and that restriction are permitted $\mathcal A$ operations.',['HENS','S19'],'adds an integration closure condition beyond the inverse-function/Hensel claim','inverse-function|formal|integration')
add('N65',s,39,'007–008','Elementary transcendental series belong to the flow-closed family','unpursued implication',r'The live corollary says that $\mathcal A_1$ is stable under canonical primitivation and “contient $\log(1+t_1)$, $\exp t_1$, $\cos t_1$, $\sin t_1$” and tangent. These belong to the characteristic-zero integration discussion begun on scan 005, not merely to the henselian family of scan 004; the cancelled scan 008 records an earlier route through a primitive and an inverse series. The hierarchy between algebraic implicit functions and flow closure is therefore an essential part of the proposed class of functions.',['HENS'],'adds a strictly different closure requirement from the context’s Hensel statement','inverse-function|henselian|formal')
add('N66',s,39,'006','The Hausdorff formal-group construction ends at a reduction','unproved claim',r'The assertion “Cette dernière est une $\mathcal A$-loi” is followed by a deleted Pfaff-system argument and a reduction using “le th. d’Ado”, ending with an ellipsis. To complete it one must show that the formal subgroup associated to the embedded Lie algebra is an $\mathcal A$ subobject and that the resulting multiplication and homomorphisms have the claimed coefficients. The reduction itself is not that final construction.',['HENS','S19'],'adds an unfinished Lie-theoretic application not addressed by the Hua entry','formal|inverse-function|SGA')
add('N67',s,39,'007','The Pfaff converse is explicitly desired','conjecture',r'“On veut qu’on ait une réciproque” introduces h): an integrable $\mathcal A$ Pfaff system should produce an $\mathcal A$ foliation. The margin says “h) implique g), qui équivaut à h) pour $p=1$”; higher-rank integration is thus distinguished from the one-dimensional flow axiom. This is a proposed axiom or desired closure property, not a theorem proved from a)–f) on this page.',['HENS','M14'],'adds a desired integration/effectivity principle; only a methodological analogy with the effectivity-axiom lead','Pfaff|effectivity|axiom|inverse-function')
add('N68',s,39,'007; 016–017','A possible higher-rank integration test','own idea',r'A useful test of the gap between g) and h) is to seek local generators of an involutive rank-$p$ distribution whose successive flows give a formal product chart with an $\mathcal A$ transversal. The obstruction is not the existence of an ordinary characteristic-zero formal Frobenius chart, but whether the normalizations and coefficient operations used to construct it stay within the chosen family. Until that closure argument is supplied, the rank-one equivalence should not be extrapolated to all $p$.',['HENS','M14'],'adds a proposed test for the stronger integral axiom','effectivity|axiom|inverse-function','medium','the construction suggests a route, but stability under all operations needed to normalize the distribution is unproved')
add('N69',s,39,'008; comparison 007, 016','The retraction and product draft is cancelled','withdrawn in the same text',r'The entire scan 008, including “Conditions équivalentes” for a retraction and a product decomposition, is crossed out. Its relation to the live transversal criterion on scan 007 and quotient-structure discussion on scan 016 is useful evidence of reformulation, but its coordinate labels and assertions cannot be combined with those live passages as one uninterrupted proof.',['HENS','M14'],'adds the distinction between withdrawn and live formulations of effectivity','effectivity|inverse-function|formal')
add('N70',s,39,'010','Permissible substitution is constrained by the augmentation','unpursued implication',r'The margin specifies “les $f_i$ sont tels que $\varepsilon(f_i)$ nilpotent”, which is essential for a continuous substitution in the formal power-series setting. The subsequent marginal amendment concerning removal of the nilpotence condition on the outer function should be checked using the annular and constant-function hypotheses, rather than interpreted as permission to substitute arbitrary non-nilpotent arguments. This distinction is exactly what reappears in the failed evaluation at 1 on scan 012.',['HENS'],'adds the formal-topology restriction behind the Hua substitution hypothesis','inverse-function|nilpotent|substitution')
add('N71',s,39,'011','Projective and finite modules are expressly queried','self-flagged',r'The construction from finite free $k_0$-modules has the parenthesis “et = projectifs de type fini?” and a less secure margin suggesting an extension to finite modules. Extending the coordinate description to projective modules requires compatible descent or idempotent splittings; extending it to arbitrary finite modules would also leave the smooth formal category used in the main paragraph. These are different extensions and should not be treated as already justified.',['HENS','M14'],'adds explicit scope questions about objects in the formal category','inverse-function|effectivity|projector|formal')
add('N72',s,39,'011','Transversal fiber products need a construction in the chosen class','unproved claim',r'“Il y a des produits finis … et des produits fibrés, dans le cas ‘transversal’” is stated before the later inverse-function condition is formulated. A proof should specify the coordinate or implicit-function argument and exactly which closure assumptions it uses, particularly for transverse equations whose solution is not polynomial. The category label read here is $\operatorname{Linc}(k_0)$, not an automatic identification with every smooth $k_0$-scheme.',['HENS','M14'],'adds a dependency check to the formal-category construction','inverse-function|formal|effectivity')
add('N73',s,39,'012','The evaluation-at-1 proof is explicitly rejected','self-flagged',r'The margin says “incorrect, 1 n’est pas nilp.! Il faut exiger une propriété de divisi-bilité” beside the passage from $f(t)^{-1}s\in\mathcal A_{n+1}$ to its value at $s=1$. Thus this written proof of the full ambient-unit assertion is not valid under the pointed substitution rule; the proposed differential/divisibility remedy must be separated from the weaker Zariskian conclusion, which has a different repair in N75. This does not by itself refute the Hua entry’s implicit-function or henselian-pair claim.',['HENS'],'differs from reading the invalid proof as complete; adds an explicit separation of the full unit assertion and the context’s weaker conclusion','inverse-function|henselian|unit')
add('N74',s,39,'012, 015','Coefficient extraction repairs the full unit assertion','own idea',r'From inverse closure applied to $(t,s)\mapsto(t,f(t)s)$ one obtains $g(t,s)=f(t)^{-1}s\in\mathcal A_{n+1}$. The coefficient-extraction consequence explicitly written on scan 015 gives $[s]g=f(t)^{-1}\in\mathcal A_n$, without substituting $s=1$. This proves the stronger ambient-unit closure under inverse closure together with that differential condition.',['HENS'],'adds a proof of the corrected strong claim with its additional hypothesis','inverse-function|differential|unit','high','the inverse coordinate is explicit and its linear coefficient is exactly the required reciprocal')
add('N75',s,39,'012–013','The Zariskian conclusion has a pointed repair using H alone','own idea',r'For $h\in\mathcal A_n$ with $h(0)=0$, the polynomial expression $(t,s)\mapsto(t,(1+h(t))(1+s)-1)$ defines a pointed formal automorphism with inverse last coordinate $(1+s)/(1+h(t))-1$. Inverse closure and the legal substitution $s=0$ give $(1+h)^{-1}-1\in\mathcal A_n$, hence $(1+h)^{-1}\in\mathcal A_n$; replacing $h$ by $ah$ for arbitrary $a\in\mathcal A_n$ proves $\mathcal A_n\cap\mathfrak m_n\subset\operatorname{rad}\mathcal A_n$. Consequently the Hua Zariskian claim can survive the manuscript’s failed proof of the stronger assertion, without the differential axiom.',['HENS'],'adds a repair supporting the restricted conclusion of 39-inverse-function-axioms-henselian','inverse-function|henselian|Zariskian','high','the map and inverse are pointed, and all substitutions used have zero constant term')
add('N76',s,39,'010–013, 015','A family separates ambient-unit closure from H','own idea',r'Over $k=\mathbb Q$, take $\mathcal A_n=\mathbb Z+\mathfrak m_n\mathbb Q[[t_1,\ldots,t_n]]$ and $\mathcal A_0=\mathbb Z$: these are unital subrings containing the coordinates and stable under all permitted zero-constant substitutions. Every pointed formal automorphism and its inverse have coordinate series of constant term zero, so inverse closure holds, but the ambient unit $2$ has reciprocal $1/2\notin\mathcal A_n$. The family fails differential closure, since $t/2$ belongs and its derivative $1/2$ does not; this distinguishes the full Corollary 1 from the Zariskian conclusion, rather than refuting the latter.',['HENS'],'adds a counterexample to the stronger ambient-unit inference; does not contradict the Hua henselian-pair conclusion','inverse-function|unit|differential','high','all nonconstant rational series are allowed, making substitution and inverse closure immediate, while the constant subring is visibly not inverse-closed in Q')
render()
print('Saved',len(load()),'notes')
